air/OS (Haiku arm64): Mesa 26.2.4's PowerVR Vulkan driver on the native
"powervr" kernel driver (/dev/graphics/powervr/0, Cubie A7S BXM-4-64 MC1).

The kernel takes Linux's drm.h / pvr_drm.h structures unchanged behind op
codes PVR_HAIKU_OP(nr) (headers/private/graphics/powervr/pvr_haiku.h in the
air/OS tree, put on the include path by build.sh, not copied). All changes are
#ifdef __HAIKU__ or Haiku-only meson except two in pvr_physical_device.c, a
NULL check and an assert fix; a Linux build of this tree is otherwise
unchanged (build.sh builds the host tools and the drm-shim smoke test from
it).

  include/drm-uapi/drm.h            BSD ioctl encoding from <bsd/sys/ioccom.h>
  include/drm-uapi/pvr_drm.h        no <linux/const.h>, <linux/types.h>: _BITULL
  include/xf86drm.h                 libdrm stand-in (Haiku only, #include_next
                                    elsewhere): drmIoctl() -> ioctl(fd,
                                    PVR_HAIKU_OP(req & 0xff), arg,
                                    IOCPARM_LEN(req)), retried on EINTR/EAGAIN;
                                    drmGetVersion/FreeVersion/GetCap/DropMaster;
                                    drmSyncobj* (create, destroy, wait, reset,
                                    signal, timeline signal/wait, query,
                                    transfer) as libdrm, but -1 + errno on
                                    failure and ETIME for every timeout; PRIME
                                    and syncobj/sync-file fds fail with
                                    EOPNOTSUPP until pvr_haiku.h names their
                                    ops; drmDevice types, an empty
                                    drmGetDevices2(); static asserts that the
                                    op numbers and sizes match pvr_haiku.h
  src/util/u_sync_provider_haiku.c  the Vulkan runtime's syncobj provider on
  src/util/u_sync_provider.h          those wrappers, timelines when the kernel
  src/util/meson.build                answers DRM_CAP_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE
  src/vulkan/runtime/meson.build    build vk_drm_syncobj.c without libdrm
  src/vulkan/runtime/vk_image.[ch]  keep drm_format_mod (pvr_image.c and
                                    EXT_image_drm_format_modifier use it)
  pvr_instance.c                    no device list: open HAIKU_PVR_DEVICE or
                                    /dev/graphics/powervr/0, accept it if
                                    DRM_IOCTL_VERSION says "powervr", describe
                                    it as a platform device with a render node
                                    only; driver build hash from the version and
                                    the loaded library file's size and mtime
                                    (dladdr + stat; the version alone if that
                                    fails): no build-id note at run time
  pvr_physical_device.c             VK_EXT_physical_device_drm: render 0:0, no
                                    primary node (stat() of a NULL path is
                                    skipped); and an upstream bug (also in
                                    main): without the shader cache,
                                    pvr_physical_device_free_pipeline_cache()
                                    asserted disk_cache != NULL and so aborted
                                    every vkDestroyInstance in a build with
                                    assertions (debugoptimized)
  pvr_arch_job_transfer.c           pvr_queue_transfer()'s submission
                                    (some 380 KiB: the prepare array) on the
                                    heap, not the submitting thread's stack
                                    (256 KiB by default on Haiku)
  winsys/powervr/pvr_drm_job_*.c    a request failing with EIO (the kernel
                                    ended the context: a GPU reset, a faulty
                                    context) or ENODEV is VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST,
                                    not VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY (render,
                                    compute, transfer, null-job syncobjs)
  pvr_arch_queue.c                  on VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST the queue (and so
                                    the device) is marked lost, which the
                                    runtime does not do for immediate-mode
                                    submits, and the submission's signals are
                                    signalled: zink waited forever on the GL
                                    thread for a batch point the failed submit
                                    would never reach
  winsys/powervr/pvr_drm_bo.c       CPU maps through PVR_HAIKU_NR_MAP_BO (a
                                    clone of the buffer's area; placed maps
                                    free the reservation and pass the address),
                                    unmapped with delete_area(); "reserve"
                                    unmaps leave a PROT_NONE mapping
  pvr_host_cache.h (new),            a second memory type, DEVICE_LOCAL |
  pvr_physical_device.c,            HOST_VISIBLE | HOST_CACHED (not COHERENT),
  pvr_device.c, winsys/pvr_winsys.h, after the coherent one (so apps asking
  winsys/powervr/pvr_drm_bo.[ch]    for HOST_VISIBLE or HOST_COHERENT still
                                    get that first): mapped write-back cached
                                    (PVR_HAIKU_MAP_BO_CACHED, through the new
                                    PVR_WINSYS_BO_FLAG_CPU_CACHED), kept
                                    coherent by vkFlushMappedMemoryRanges
                                    ("dc cvac") and
                                    vkInvalidateMappedMemoryRanges ("dc
                                    civac"), which were no-ops;
                                    nonCoherentAtomSize is the larger of the
                                    cache line (CTR_EL0) and 64.
                                    PVR_CACHED_MEMORY_TYPE=0 hides the type
                                    (=1 shows it off Haiku); a static_assert
                                    checks the 32-byte struct
                                    pvr_haiku_map_bo with its flags
  include/vk_haiku_scanout.h (new), VkImportScanoutMemoryHAIKU (sType
  pvr_device.c, winsys/pvr_winsys.h, 1000399000), Summit's direct present's
  winsys/powervr/pvr_drm.c,         way to the screen: vkAllocateMemory with
  winsys/powervr/pvr_drm_bo.[ch]    it in pNext (allocationSize ignored, any
                                    device-local type) imports the
                                    sunxi_display frame buffer: GET_STATE
                                    for its shape, CLONE_FRAME_BUFFER into
                                    this team, PVR_HAIKU_NR_IMPORT_HOST of the
                                    clone with DRM_PVR_BO_BYPASS_DEVICE_CACHE
                                    (no GPU cache line half over a
                                    rectangle's edge); width, height,
                                    rowPitch and size are written back. The
                                    buffer object owns the clone: GEM_CLOSE,
                                    then delete_area(); its CPU map is the
                                    clone. Without the display driver, or a
                                    kernel without the import, it fails and
                                    zink presents some other way. The memory
                                    object is made after the import, with
                                    the buffer object's size (zink passes
                                    allocationSize 0, which the runtime
                                    asserts on). A new winsys op,
                                    buffer_import_scanout; sunxi_display.h
                                    comes by path, like pvr_haiku.h. Off
                                    Haiku, PVR_SHIM_SCANOUT=WxH makes a
                                    stand-in buffer object instead, for
                                    build.sh shim; without it there is no
                                    frame buffer
  pvr_rt_dataset.[ch], pvr_device.h, a device cache of idle render target data
  pvr_arch_device.c,                sets (HWRT data set + local free list) for
  pvr_arch_cmd_buffer.c             dynamic rendering. Every
                                    vkCmdBeginRendering() (zink's every render
                                    pass) has its own render state, and so
                                    created both and destroyed them when the
                                    command buffer was reset: firmware objects,
                                    buffers, VM maps and synchronous firmware
                                    cleanups in the kernel, every frame. A data
                                    set still belongs to one render state at a
                                    time; once that is cleaned up (its command
                                    buffer no longer pending) the next
                                    rendering of the same width, height,
                                    samples and layers takes it. At most 16
                                    idle ones are kept, and one is destroyed
                                    after 256 later releases (old sizes age
                                    out); one left mid-render (need_frag) is
                                    destroyed. Render pass framebuffers keep
                                    their own data sets, as before

Not changed: the heap size (util/os_misc.c: sysconf(_SC_PHYS_PAGES), which
Haiku's libroot answers from get_system_info()); the conformance gate
(PVR_I_WANT_A_BROKEN_VULKAN_DRIVER=1); bxm-4-64.h (26.2.4's is identical to
Mesa main's, gs_rta_support off and usc_alu_roundingmode_rne on); mutexes
(every mutex in src/imagination is initialized explicitly).

